{"claim_id": "CT-S9-PVPARAMS-C006", "task_id": "CT-S9-PVPARAMS", "claim": "The finite singularities of System C are -1/6 - sqrt(11)I/6 and -1/6 + sqrt(11)I/6.", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "scripts\\compute_system_c_local_data.py", "output_hash": "2909D84BEACB1DC71B4310037D78F1A2174345074A580247AD73E927651C8D91", "label": "PROVEN"}
{"claim_id": "CT-S9-PVPARAMS-C007", "task_id": "CT-S9-PVPARAMS", "claim": "At both finite singularities the indicial polynomial is lambda^2, hence the scalar local exponents are {0,0} and exponent difference is 0.", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "scripts\\compute_system_c_local_data.py", "output_hash": "2909D84BEACB1DC71B4310037D78F1A2174345074A580247AD73E927651C8D91", "label": "PROVEN"}
{"claim_id": "CT-S9-PVPARAMS-C008", "task_id": "CT-S9-PVPARAMS", "claim": "The second Frobenius solution has nonzero log coefficient sqrt(11)I/11 at -1/6 - sqrt(11)I/6 and -sqrt(11)I/11 at -1/6 + sqrt(11)I/6 when y1(s)=1.", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "scripts\\compute_system_c_local_data.py", "output_hash": "2909D84BEACB1DC71B4310037D78F1A2174345074A580247AD73E927651C8D91", "label": "PROVEN"}
{"claim_id": "CT-S9-PVPARAMS-C009", "task_id": "CT-S9-PVPARAMS", "claim": "The normal-form potential is (3*x^4+x^3+x^2+11/4)/(9*x^4+6*x^3+7*x^2+2*x+1), equivalently (12*x^4+4*x^3+4*x^2+11)/(4*(3*x^2+x+1)^2).", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "scripts\\compute_system_c_local_data.py", "output_hash": "2909D84BEACB1DC71B4310037D78F1A2174345074A580247AD73E927651C8D91", "label": "PROVEN"}
{"claim_id": "CT-S9-PVPARAMS-C010", "task_id": "CT-S9-PVPARAMS", "claim": "At both finite singularities the coefficient of (x-s)^-2 in the normal-form potential is -1/4, so the normal-form exponent is repeated at 1/2.", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "scripts\\compute_system_c_local_data.py", "output_hash": "2909D84BEACB1DC71B4310037D78F1A2174345074A580247AD73E927651C8D91", "label": "PROVEN"}
{"claim_id": "CT-S9-PVPARAMS-C011", "task_id": "CT-S9-PVPARAMS", "claim": "At infinity, in t=1/x, the normal-form potential has pole order 4, leading coefficient 1/3, no ramification, and Katz invariant 1.", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "scripts\\compute_system_c_local_data.py", "output_hash": "2909D84BEACB1DC71B4310037D78F1A2174345074A580247AD73E927651C8D91", "label": "PROVEN"}
{"claim_id": "CT-S9-PVPARAMS-C012", "task_id": "CT-S9-PVPARAMS", "claim": "The scalar normal-form exponential factors at infinity begin exp((sqrt(3)/3)*x) and exp((-sqrt(3)/3)*x), equivalently exp(-(sqrt(3)/3)/t) and exp((sqrt(3)/3)/t), with Riccati log coefficients recorded in the output file.", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "scripts\\compute_system_c_local_data.py", "output_hash": "2909D84BEACB1DC71B4310037D78F1A2174345074A580247AD73E927651C8D91", "label": "PROVEN"}
